<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salween</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thanlwin, Nu River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Salween is a major river of South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, rising on the Tibetan Plateau and flowing south through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, the Shan and Karenni hills of Myanmar, and along part of the Thai border to the Andaman Sea at Mawlamyine. Its deep, rapid course is largely unnavigable, and for centuries it formed a frontier between Burmese, Shan and Tai states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salween.jpg" alt="Salween" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saosukham · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sweet_View_of_Salween_River_in_Tang_Yan_Township,_Shan_State,_Myanmar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
